"{\"content\":\"Semantics: abs — Small-Step Operational Semantics\\n\\n1. Abstract Syntax\\n    Expressions  e ::= n            integer literals, n in Z\\n                 |  abs(e)          absolute value application\\n                 |  e1 + e2         integer addition\\n\\n    Values       v ::= n            (a subset of expressions)\\n\\n2. Evaluation Relation\\n    The small-step evaluation relation  ->  is the smallest binary\\n    relation on expressions such that every instance of the inference\\n    rules below holds.\\n\\n        Notation:   e -> e'      \\\"e steps to e' in one step\\\"\\n                    e ->* e'     reflexive-transitive closure of ->\\n\\n3. Inference Rules\\n\\n    --- Congruence (evaluation contexts) ---\\n\\n    (S-ABS)                 e  ->  e'\\n                        -----------------\\n                        abs(e) -> abs(e')\\n\\n    (S-ADD-L)     e1 -> e1'\\n                -----------------\\n                e1 + e2 -> e1' + e2\\n\\n    (S-ADD-R)     e2 -> e2'\\n                -----------------\\n                n + e2 -> n + e2'\\n\\n    --- Reduction (computation) rules ---\\n\\n    (R-ADD)    n1 + n2 -> n3        where n3 = n1 + n2   (integer sum)\\n\\n    (R-ABS-POS)   abs(n) -> n       if n >= 0\\n\\n    (R-ABS-NEG)   abs(n) -> n'      if n < 0  and  n' = -n\\n\\n4. Derived Properties\\n\\n    P1 (Progress)     If e is a closed, well-formed expression, then\\n                      either e is a value or there exists e' with\\n                      e -> e'.\\n\\n    P2 (Determinism)  If e -> e1 and e -> e2, then e1 = e2.\\n                      (The side conditions on R-ABS-POS and\\n                      R-ABS-NEG are exclusive and exhaustive.)\\n\\n    P3 (Termination)  Every closed expression e reduces to a value:\\n                      there exists n with e ->* n. Each step strictly\\n                      decreases expression size; rules never enlarge\\n                      terms.\\n\\n5. Correspondence with the Specification (spec.txt)\\n\\n    The reduction rules R-ABS-POS and R-ABS-NEG realize exactly the\\n    defining clauses of abs:\\n\\n        R-ABS-POS  realizes  abs(x) =  x   if x >= 0\\n        R-ABS-NEG  realizes  abs(x) = -x   if x <  0\\n\\n    Consequently, for every closed expression e that reduces to n:\\n\\n        abs(n) ->* m   with  m >= 0  and  m = n if n >= 0, m = -n otherwise\\n\\n    i.e. m satisfies properties P1-P3 of spec.txt.\\n\\n6. Example Derivation\\n\\n    abs(-3 + 1)\\n      -> abs(-2)          by S-ADD-L? no: S-ADD-L on inner sum (-3 -> -2 is\\n                          a value step; here -3 + 1 -> -2 by R-ADD)\\n      -> -2               by R-ABS-NEG\\n\",\"path\":\"semantics.txt\"}"